# ── proptest counterexample corpora — TRACKED DELIBERATELY, both layouts ──
#
# The rule this replaces asserted these files were "safe to regenerate, not worth
# tracking". ⚠ THAT CLAIM WAS FALSE, and it is the reason it is spelled out here rather
# than simply deleted. A proptest corpus records seeds for inputs that ONCE FALSIFIED A
# PROPERTY, together with the shrunk counterexample. Regenerating one means re-running the
# random search that originally found it — an unbounded number of cases, with no guarantee
# the same input is ever drawn again, and no guarantee at all once the surrounding types
# drift. Deleting the file discards a witness; it does not schedule its recreation.
#
# proptest says so itself, in the header it writes into every one of these files verbatim:
#
#     "It is recommended to check this file in to source control so that
#      everyone who runs the test benefits from these saved cases."
#
# Two DISTINCT on-disk layouts exist, and a rule for one does not cover the other. The old
# rule matched only layout B, so layout A was never ignored — which is why
# `rspace++/proptest-regressions/hot_store_spec.txt` is tracked while
# `models/tests/epathmap_spliced_event_bytes.proptest-regressions` was not. Both layouts
# are un-ignored here, by name:
#
#   LAYOUT B — `<dir>/<name>.proptest-regressions`
#     proptest's `FileFailurePersistence::Direct` form, with the corpus path pinned
#     explicitly by the test's `ProptestConfig`.
#
#   LAYOUT A — `<crate>/proptest-regressions/<module path>.txt`
#     proptest's DEFAULT `FileFailurePersistence::SourceParallel` form, derived from
#     `file!()`.
#
# ⚠ `.gitignore` has no effect on files git already tracks: un-ignoring changes the
# behaviour only for files that are currently UNTRACKED.
!**/*.proptest-regressions
!**/proptest-regressions/
!**/proptest-regressions/**

